				\begin{document}$${E}_{u}$$\end{document} ) increased, indicating higher disorder levels in the material. The improved structural, optical, and electrical characteristics of PVA/PVP–GnP nanocomposites underscore their potential for high-performance applications in optoelectronics, energy storage, and photonic technologies.

For the preparation of PVA/PVP (80/20 wt%)–GnP nanocomposite dispersions, 1.92 g of PVA and 0.48 g of PVP powders were first dissolved separately in 30 mL of double-distilled water at 70 °C under magnetic stirring until complete dissolution was achieved. Stirring was continued until the solutions cooled to room temperature. The obtained solutions were then combined under magnetic stirring to form a homogeneous PVA/PVP hybrid polymer stock solution. Subsequently, this homogeneous solution was divided into six smaller vials, into which varying amounts of GnP, determined according to the equation given below, were added. 1 ϕ = m GnP / ρ GnP m GnP / ρ GnP + m PVA / ρ PVA + m PVP / ρ PVP \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
