The wavelength-dependent variation in optical conductivity for all samples is shown in Fig.  8 . The figure indicates an increase in optical conductivity as the wavelength decreases for all samples. This increase can be attributed to the fact that photons with shorter wavelengths and higher energy are more effective at exciting the material's electrons. As the material absorbs more photons, the absorbance and absorption coefficient increase. Consequently, the optical conductivity rises, as described by Eq. ( 10 ). In the low-wavelength region, the photon energy is sufficient to excite electrons from the valence band to the conduction band; as a result, a significant increase in optical conductivity is observed. The addition of GnP nanoparticles into the hybrid matrix increases the density of localized states within the band gap, thereby enhancing both the absorption coefficient and the optical conductivity of the nanocomposites [ 32 ]. This enhancement is associated with the formation of charge transfer complexes between GnP and the PVA/PVP hybrid matrix, as well as the increase in the number of free charge carriers due to doping. Furthermore, the decrease in the crystallinity of the polymer films reduces the optical bandgap, contributing to higher optical conductivity. Structural irregularities and the increase in defect states facilitate electron mobility within the band gap, further boosting optical conductivity [ 13 ,  24 ]. The high and stable  σ opt \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
